{0 Pipelines}

{!Eon_ecs.Pipeline.Default} groups systems into named {b phases} and
orders the phases relative to each other. It's the layer that turns a pile
of individually-callable systems (previous chapter) into one coherent tick.
Examples below are from the companion
{{:https://github.com/imunitic/eon/blob/main/eon_ecs/examples/book/pipelines_example.ml}[pipelines_example.ml]}.

{1 Phases and ordering}

Phases are polymorphic variants — any value works. Ordering is declared
between {b phases}, not individual systems: every system in an earlier
phase runs before any system in a later one.

{[
  module Pipeline = Eon_ecs.Pipeline.Default

  let build_pipeline spawned =
    Pipeline.create ()
    |> Pipeline.add_phase `Input
    |> Pipeline.add_phase `Gameplay
    |> Pipeline.before ~earlier:`Input ~later:`Gameplay
    |> Pipeline.add_system `Input    (input_system spawned)
    |> Pipeline.add_system `Gameplay (movement_system ())
]}

[Pipeline.before]/[after] say the same thing from either direction — pick
whichever reads more naturally at the call site. {b Never add phases or
ordering edges after the pipeline starts running.} The topological sort is
cached and only re-runs when a phase or edge is added — adding a system
does not invalidate the cache, so a phase/edge added after the first tick
silently has no effect on ordering.

{1 register_all: what it automates}

The previous chapter showed [System.register] and [System.attach] called
by hand, one system at a time. [Pipeline.register_all] does both, for
{b every system in the pipeline}, in topological phase order, in one call:

{[
  let world    = make_world () in
  let pipeline = build_pipeline (ref []) in

  let e = World.create_entity world in
  World.add_component world e ~name:Position.name { Position.x = 0.0; y = 0.0 };
  World.add_component world e ~name:Velocity.name { Velocity.dx = 1.0; dy = 0.0 };

  Pipeline.register_all pipeline world;

  let world = Pipeline.run pipeline world 1.0 in
  match World.get_component world e ~name:Position.name with
  | Some (p : Position.t) -> assert (p.x = 1.0)
  | None -> assert false
]}

[Pipeline.run] executes every system's [update] in topological phase
order, ignoring [kind] — it's what {!Eon_ecs.Progress} calls internally,
usually you'll drive a pipeline through a [Progress] controller (next
chapter) rather than calling [run] directly.

{1 register_all is not idempotent}

Calling [register_all] a second time without resetting first raises — the
pipeline refuses to silently double-attach every handler:

{[
  let world    = make_world () in
  let pipeline = build_pipeline (ref []) in
  Pipeline.register_all pipeline world;
  match Pipeline.register_all pipeline world with
  | () -> assert false
  | exception Invalid_argument _ -> ()
]}

{1 reset: the scene-transition pattern}

[Pipeline.reset] clears every bus subscriber and lets [register_all] run
again. The intended shape is: tear down a scene, rebuild the pipeline (or
reuse the same one), [reset], then [register_all] for the next scene:

{[
  Pipeline.register_all pipeline world;
  Pipeline.reset pipeline;
  Pipeline.register_all pipeline world   (* no longer raises *)
]}

